<p data-start=3D"1238" data-end=3D"1261">their own proving key</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1262" data-end=3D"1289">
<p data-start=3D"1264" data-end=3D"1289">their own settings.json</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p data-start=3D"1291" data-end=3D"1360">In principle, you <strong data-sta=
rt=3D"1309" data-end=3D"1316">can</strong> submit any circuit you want <str=
ong data-start=3D"1345" data-end=3D"1359">as long as</strong>:</p>
<h3 data-start=3D"1362" data-end=3D"1406">=E2=9C=94 The model has the corre=
ct input type</h3>
<h3 data-start=3D"1407" data-end=3D"1457">=E2=9C=94 The model returns the c=
orrect output shape</h3>
<h3 data-start=3D"1458" data-end=3D"1484">=E2=9C=94 The proof is valid</h3>
<p data-start=3D"1486" data-end=3D"1502">But in practice:</p>
<h3 data-start=3D"1504" data-end=3D"1551">=E2=9D=97 Custom circuits <em dat=
a-start=3D"1526" data-end=3D"1549">do NOT give advantage</em></h3>
<h3 data-start=3D"1552" data-end=3D"1598">=E2=9D=97 Smaller models <em data=
-start=3D"1573" data-end=3D"1596">do NOT increase score</em></h3>
<h3 data-start=3D"1599" data-end=3D"1651">=E2=9D=97 More accurate models <e=
m data-start=3D"1626" data-end=3D"1649">do NOT increase score</em></h3>
<p data-start=3D"1653" data-end=3D"1681">Validators only verify that:</p>
<ol data-start=3D"1683" data-end=3D"1808">
<li data-start=3D"1683" data-end=3D"1723">
<p data-start=3D"1686" data-end=3D"1723">Your input witness + proof is vali=
d</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1724" data-end=3D"1776">
<p data-start=3D"1727" data-end=3D"1776">The outputs match what the circuit=
 would output</p>
</li>
<li data-start=3D"1777" data-end=3D"1808">
<p data-start=3D"1780" data-end=3D"1808">The circuit runs correctly</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p data-start=3D"1810" data-end=3D"1856">There is <strong data-start=3D"181=
9" data-end=3D"1840">no ML competition</strong> happening here.</p>
